# Global Instructions

Portable global instructions for Claude Code: how to work, how memory is organised, which tool or
agent to reach for. Nothing here is specific to one person, machine or project. Keep it that way:
a status log, hostnames or pending tasks do not belong in a file that loads in full every session;
put those in memory or project notes.

## Memory: four layers, one owner each

Read this before touching anything memory-shaped. Several tools overlap; each owns exactly one
layer, and failures come from letting them bleed into each other.

| Layer | Question it answers | Lives in | Loaded | Written by |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **1 Instructions** | How do I work here? | `CLAUDE.md`, `.claude/rules/` | every session, in full | the user |
| **2 Durable facts** | What did we learn? What was decided? What trap cost a day? | `~/.claude/projects/<repo>/memory/*.md` -> `MEMORY.md` | index at start, files on demand | the agent, curated, plus an automatic backstop |
| **3 Project state** | Where does this project stand right now? | `.planning/`, committed in the repo | on demand | phase commands (GSD) |
| **4 Session activity** | What just happened? What was I doing before compaction? | claude-mem observations; context-mode session guide | injected at start / after compaction | automatic |

**The boundary rule between 2 and 3, which stops both from rotting:**

> Layer 3 is mutable and always current: it gets overwritten. Layer 2 is immutable and dated: it
> gets appended. "The task table is empty" is *state*. "Task state moved to Postgres
> because JSON files lost writes under concurrency" is a *fact*. If a sentence will be false next
> week, it belongs in `.planning/STATE.md` and never in a memory file.

**Layer 2 has a deterministic backstop, not just judgment in the moment.** A `SessionEnd` hook
(`~/.claude/hooks/memory-auto-capture.mjs`, registered globally, so it covers every project with
no per-project setup) runs a cheap `claude -p --model haiku` pass at the end of every session
through the existing subscription. It re-reads the transcript against this exact contract and
writes anything durable the live session did not capture. It is silent when there is nothing to
write, which is most sessions. Assume it is running, and still write a fact yourself when you are
already sure of it; the hook is a safety net, not a reason to skip.

`PreCompact` gets the same pass, and `PostToolUse` (Write/Edit/MultiEdit) adds a 15-minute
throttled safety net so a hard crash loses at most one window. All three share a 2-minute
cross-trigger debounce, added after a stray process restarted `claude` every minute or so and
fired `SessionEnd` dozens of times with nothing to write.

Layer 2 sits on Claude Code's own auto-memory directory, so it loads through the native path.
Three consequences that are easy to get wrong:

- **The limit is 200 lines OR 25 KB, whichever comes first**, not bytes alone. The generator
  enforces both, measuring what actually loads (frontmatter and block comments stripped first).
- **The corpus is keyed on the git repository, not the cwd**, so every worktree and subdirectory
  of one repo shares a single corpus. Resolve the root with `git rev-parse --git-common-dir`, not
  `--show-toplevel`, which returns the worktree and silently forks the corpus in two.
- **Never disable auto memory.** `MEMORY.md` loads *because* that feature is on. Turning it off to
  "avoid the overlap" switches off the entire memory system.

Concurrent sessions are safe by construction: `MEMORY.md` is **derived, never appended**, so two
sessions regenerating it converge instead of clobbering. Keep it that way: one fact per file,
named `<topic>-<YYYY-MM-DD>.md`.

## Understand the goal before building: the 95% rule

Before a non-trivial implementation (skip for one-line fixes, direct questions, or an obviously
complete spec), silently self-assess: do I understand the full intent, meaning the literal ask,
the implied requirements, the edge cases, and how it connects to the existing codebase, to better
than 95% confidence?

- **>95%**: proceed without asking. Do not manufacture questions.
- **<=95%**: ask targeted questions before writing code. Be specific about what is uncertain, and
  surface what the user probably did not think to mention (related systems, edge cases, what
  "done" means) instead of silently guessing.

A small obvious task at 98% needs zero questions; an underspecified "build me X" needs them even
if it sounds simple.

## Effort triage: how much process a task deserves

- **Trivial** (typo, one-line fix, direct question): handle directly, no subagent overhead.
- **Moderate, well-scoped** (a few files, no real unknowns): do it inline, then run the matching
  reviewer.
- **New feature or subsystem** (multiple files, real design decisions, real test surface): plan
  first (`planner`), then TDD (`tdd-guide`), then review. Write the plan down if it spans more than
  one session.

**Escalate, do not force a bad fit.** If a task reveals more complexity than judged, stop, say so
and escalate. The reverse holds too: drop the ceremony when a task turns out trivial.

### When GSD is the right lane

GSD owns **layer 3** (`.planning/`, committed) and runs research, planning and execution in
**fresh-context subagents** so the main session stays lean. That is the remedy for context rot.
Reach for it when the work is multi-session or multi-phase, or when "where did we leave off" would
otherwise be answered by scrolling:

- `/gsd-onboard`: once per repo, to create `.planning/` from an existing codebase
- `/gsd-plan-phase` -> `/gsd-execute-phase` -> `/gsd-verify-work`: a feature spanning sessions
- `/gsd-progress`: "where does this stand", answered from committed state, not memory

Not for a one-file fix, a direct question, or anything the triage above calls trivial or moderate.
Its bookkeeping costs more than it returns there. Low usage count measured onboarding, not value:
a repo that was never onboarded cannot fire it.

### GSD vs superpowers: not competitors

|  | GSD | superpowers |
|---|---|---|
| Question | Where does this project stand, and what is next? | How do I do this task well, right now? |
| Unit | a phase, spanning sessions | a single task, inside one session |
| Leaves behind | files in `.planning/`, committed | nothing; it changes how you work |
| Reach for it when | work outlives one session | you are about to debug, plan, review, or claim done |

GSD is machinery. superpowers is discipline. A one-file bug fix needs no `.planning/` phase but
absolutely needs `systematic-debugging` and `verification-before-completion`. Disciplines worth
holding even without invoking the skill:

- **verification-before-completion**: no completion claims without fresh verification evidence. If
  you have not run the command in this message, you cannot say it passes.
- **systematic-debugging**: find the root cause. A random fix that makes the symptom go away is the
  expensive path.
- **receiving-code-review**: verify the claim before implementing it; agreement is not rigour.

These are standards to hold, not rituals to perform. Do not invoke a skill to raise a number, and
ignore any plugin framing that says to invoke a skill before every response: that is what makes a
whole set read as noise.

### Delegated is not done: verify before you tabulate

A subagent's "required changes", "next steps" or "proposed values" are a **to-do list**. They say
what it recommends, not what exists. Its summary of what it built is a claim about a file you have
not read.

**Before any of these, verify each line against the code:**

1. writing a status table, a "what shipped" list, or a session summary;
2. marking a task complete, or telling the user a stage is finished;
3. answering "did we do X?" about anything earlier in the session.

Verification means one command whose output you can see: `grep` for the symbol, `wc -l` the file,
read the `package.json` key. Not "I remember adding it".

**The trap:** an agent reports "I built X and recorded the wiring in REQUIRED-CHANGES.md". X
exists; the wiring does not. Both halves arrive in one message and only one is done. In a
four-agent batch, a status table written from recollection would have asserted four false things:
an npm script never added, a devDependency never installed, a defect fixed in one caller but not
another, and a file 150 lines longer than remembered. One `grep` each caught all four. The risk is
highest right after a compaction, when a summary flattens "recommended" and "done" into the same
past tense. This is a habit of checking, not a memory-corpus job.

## Agent triage: which specialist, automatically

Fire on the signal, no prompt needed:

- Any `.tsx`/`.jsx` touched or React component logic -> `react-reviewer`
- Any `.ts`/`.js` touched -> `typescript-reviewer`
- Any `.py` touched -> `python-reviewer`
- React/Next.js build failure -> `react-build-resolver`
- Code just written or modified (general) -> `code-reviewer`
- Auth, payments, user input, other security-sensitive code -> `security-reviewer`
- SQL, migrations, schema or query changes -> `database-reviewer`
- Architectural decision (new subsystem, data model, cross-cutting change) -> `architect`
- New feature or bug fix -> `tdd-guide`
- Complex or ambiguous feature needing a plan first -> `planner`
- SEO, metadata, sitemap, structured data, Core Web Vitals -> `seo-specialist`
- UI component, design system, accessibility -> `a11y-architect`
- Silently-swallowed errors, bad fallbacks -> `silent-failure-hunter`
- Work on this Claude Code setup itself (agents, skills, hooks, MCP) -> `harness-optimizer`

**Model dial**: default to each agent's own declared `model:` tier. Escalate one invocation only
when the change is unusually high-stakes. **These are triggers, not a quota**: a one-line edit does
not need a reviewer, and most subagent calls should go to the two built-ins (`general-purpose`,
`Explore`) unless a specific reviewer fits.

## code-review-graph: auto-setup for new projects

The CLI needs one-time per-project activation. When starting substantive work in a project with
real source code and no `.code-review-graph/` directory, run without waiting to be asked:

```
code-review-graph install --platform claude-code
code-review-graph build
```

Skip for throwaway scripts, single-file tasks, or tiny repos. Once built, its own hooks handle
incremental updates and the pre-commit refresh.

## MCP tool triage: which server, automatically

Skills carry their own descriptions, so no per-skill rule is needed. MCP tools are just present in
the tool list with no "notice and reach for me" instinct, which is why they need explicit rows.
Extend this table, do not write new prose, and only add a row once the tool has proven itself.

| Trigger | Tool | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Library/framework/API usage, setup, version-specific behaviour | `context7` | `resolve-library-id` then `get-library-docs`. Skip for trivial or stdlib questions. |
| UI/UX visual design, a component's look, "how do real apps solve this" | `mobbin` | Use a live reference instead of guessing. Skip for backend work. |
| Cross-repo code question, "how does X work here", onboarding a repo | `greptile` + `gbrain` | Whichever answers faster. Greptile needs `index_repository` once per repo. |
| Verifying a live page really renders or behaves as claimed | `playwright` | Run headless. |

A rule works when it names a concrete trigger instead of ordering a tool to be used before
everything.

## Web: WebSearch / WebFetch by default, a crawler for four jobs

Default to `WebSearch` and `WebFetch`. Reach for Crawl4AI (`md`, `crawl`, `html`, `screenshot`,
`pdf`, `execute_js`, `ask`) only when the task needs one of:

- a whole site or many pages crawled, not one page read
- a JS-rendered page that a plain fetch returns as an empty shell
- structured CSS/XPath/schema extraction, not a summary
- a screenshot or PDF of a page

It needs a local container; if unreachable, fall back to `WebFetch` and say so. `agent-reach`
owns login-walled social platforms (X, Reddit, YouTube, LinkedIn, Instagram, ...) because it reuses
the logged-in browser session; a headless crawler has no cookies for those and hits the same walls.
Most web questions are one page or one search, so do not reach past a plain fetch without one of
the four reasons above.

## Memory: one file per fact, and never hand-edit the index

`MEMORY.md` is a **generated file**. Hand-edits are erased on the next run, and worse, it is a
read-modify-write on a shared file with no lock: with two sessions running, the slower writer
silently erases the faster one's entry. The fact file survives but becomes unreachable from the
only map anything has. Where a generic memory instruction says to add a pointer to `MEMORY.md`,
do not.

### How to record a memory

1. **Write a NEW file** in the project's memory directory
   (`~/.claude/projects/<cwd-with-non-alphanumerics-as-hyphens>/memory/`). Never edit `MEMORY.md`
   and never edit another session's file.
2. Name it `<topic>-<YYYY-MM-DD>.md`. The frontmatter `name` must equal the filename without
   `.md`; the generator fails loudly if it does not.
3. Fill the frontmatter contract:

```yaml
---
name: payments-backups-2026-07-30       # == filename, minus .md
title: Backups, 2026-07-30              # optional; shown in the index
description: >-                         # THIS BECOMES THE INDEX LINE. Max 240 chars.
  The one fact you would regret forgetting. Write it as the hook, not as a title.
metadata:
  type: project                         # user | feedback | project | reference
  section: current                      # must exist in that project's sections.json
  date: 2026-07-30                      # when the fact was established
  verify_by: 2027-01-01                 # OPTIONAL, but use it for anything that rots
  status: deployed                      # deployed | pushed-not-merged | committed-not-pushed
                                        # | uncommitted | plan-only | superseded
---
```

4. **Run the generator** from the project directory:

```
node ~/.claude/scripts/memory-index.mjs
```

   It rebuilds `MEMORY.md`, validates the corpus, flags anything past its `verify_by`, and refuses
   to write an index large enough to stop being loaded. Fix whatever it reports; do not work
   around it. `--check` validates without writing. In a project whose memories predate the
   contract, the first run upgrades them in place and preserves everything written. The 240-char
   limit is a per-section default a project's `sections.json` may raise (`{"id":"current",
   "maxChars":400}`). If you change the generator, run its tests with explicit file names:
   `node --test ~/.claude/scripts/*.test.mjs` (the bare directory form fails on Node 22).

5. **Commit the memory directory**: `git -C <memory dir> add -A -- . && git commit -- .`. The
   generator creates the repo on first run. The `-- .` is load-bearing: with no pathspec,
   `add -A` stages the entire outer repo when the memory folder is nested inside a project.
6. **Push, if the memory dir has an `origin`**:
   `git -C <memory dir> remote get-url origin >/dev/null 2>&1 && git -C <memory dir> push || true`.
   No remote is a normal no-op.

### Where the memory folder can live

- **Default, machine-local** (`~/.claude/projects/<repo>/memory/`): isolated, private, invisible
  to other machines and to cloud sessions.
- **In-repo** (`<repo>/.claude/memory/`): commits and pushes with the code, so a cloud session sees
  it. The generator and hook detect that the folder is already inside another git working tree and
  defer; they never create a second repo there (a nested `.git` inside a tracked directory is a
  broken gitlink and silently stops tracking the files).
- **Dedicated private repo per project**: the machine-local directory is already a git repo, so
  giving it an `origin` and pushing is all it takes. Memory is then never only on one machine and
  does not pollute code history.

Choose the dedicated repo by default, and embed in-repo only when the project needs a cloud
session to read and write memory.

### Finding and retiring memories

The index is capped on purpose, so the cold half is reached by search:

```
node ~/.claude/scripts/memory-index.mjs --search <query>   # every memory, archive included
node ~/.claude/scripts/memory-index.mjs --audit            # corpus review packet
```

To retire a memory without losing it: `git mv <file> archive/`, then re-run the generator. The
generator ignores `archive/`, so it costs no index bytes, but `--search` still reaches it.

#### Search the corpus at these five moments

The index is the half you get for free. The traps, dead ends and "we tried that" live in the files
and archive, reachable only by asking. A corpus you only write to is not memory: one bug cost a
long detour and a wrong diagnosis because a memory written weeks earlier already named the cause
(a smooth-scroll library overriding programmatic scroll) and was never consulted.

1. **Before the second attempt at any bug.** The first attempt is cheap; once one fails, ask the
   corpus before running another experiment.
2. **When a tool, test or library behaves in a way that surprises you.** Surprise is the signal
   that someone already met this.
3. **Before saying "this is a new problem" or re-deriving a root cause.**
4. **Before re-running an audit, investigation or migration** that may have been done. Several
   memories exist purely to say "do not redo this, it was disproven".
5. **Before writing a new memory**, to edit the existing file instead. A correction is worth more
   than a second memory that half-contradicts the first, and costs zero index bytes.

Search ONE noun at a time (`ECONNRESET`, `deadlock`, `42P07`). It is a literal substring search: a
two-word phrase matches only if those exact words are adjacent.

#### The corpus speaks first when a command fails

`~/.claude/hooks/memory-recall-on-failure.mjs` is wired to `PostToolUseFailure` (Bash). When a
command fails it takes the distinctive words from the error, matches them against every memory
including the archive, and injects at most 2 hits. What to preserve if you touch it:

- **The description is the signal.** A term in the curated one-line description is a claim about
  what the memory is for; the same term deep in a body is a coincidence. Description matches
  qualify alone; body-only matches need two independent rare tokens.
- **Silence is the default.** On realistic failures it surfaced the right memory for a migration
  error and a connection-pool error, and said nothing for a plain test failure or a missing module.
- Capped at 2, debounced an hour per memory, and harmless: it runs after the command already
  failed and only appends text. Every fire is logged to `~/.claude/logs/memory-recall.log`; if it
  fires constantly and is never relevant, delete it.

#### Archiving happens on its own, and what it will not touch

`~/.claude/scripts/memory-autoarchive.mjs` runs weekly over every project corpus. It exists
because a warning at 17 KB is only a warning, and past the ceiling the index stops loading and
**every** memory becomes unreachable at once: a cliff, not a slope. It acts only above 19 KB,
stops at 15 KB, and **archives, never deletes**. It skips, whatever their size or age:

- `status: uncommitted | plan-only | committed-not-pushed | pushed-not-merged` (live state nobody
  can re-derive);
- `type: user | feedback` (standing instructions do not go stale by ageing);
- anything under 30 days;
- anything with `pin: true` in its metadata.

Use `pin: true` on a memory that is a standing reference rather than a record of work: a charter,
a legal position, a rule. Ranking is oldest-first and cannot tell those from a settled batch
record. Each run is one commit, so `git revert` undoes it whole; preview with
`--dry-run --verbose`. After it runs, that corpus's `MEMORY.md` is stale until the generator runs
again from inside the project.

Run the `memory-audit` skill quarterly, or whenever two memories seem to disagree. It is the only
thing that catches contradictions; `verify_by` catches age, not two memories that conflict from the
day they are written. The SessionStart hook warns past 17 KB in *memories remaining* rather than
bytes, because "about 13 more" is a deadline and "3.6 KB" is not.

### Backing up: the corpus cannot be re-derived

Code is on a remote and the database is backed up; decisions, traps and underivable status exist
only in these files.

```
node ~/.claude/scripts/memory-backup.mjs           # every project, one encrypted object
node ~/.claude/scripts/memory-restore-verify.mjs   # the drill; exits non-zero on mismatch
```

Each corpus goes in as `git bundle --all` (full history, so a bad edit is recoverable), plus the
harness that reads it, tarred, `age`-encrypted, and uploaded to object storage. The store only ever
receives ciphertext, and the script refuses to run unencrypted. Harness files come from an
**explicit allowlist, never a glob**, because the credentials file sits beside them. A scheduled
job runs it weekly. Restoring needs the age private key, deliberately not stored beside the
ciphertext config. **Uncommitted memories are not in any backup** (a bundle holds committed
history), so the script names dirty corpora instead of quietly protecting less than it appears to.

### Set `verify_by` on anything that can rot

Prices, tariffs, third-party API behaviour, competitor facts, legal thresholds, free-tier limits.
Without it, two memories written months apart eventually contradict each other and nothing flags
it. Past the date, the index shows a verify warning at session start.

### Record only what cannot be derived

If `git log`, the database or the code can answer it, do not write it down. Memory is for:

1. **Decisions and their reasons**: "no consent banner, decided, because ..."
2. **Traps that cost real time**: "the PDF renderer silently substitutes a fallback font"
3. **Status nobody can derive**: "migration 0009 must be applied by hand"

Everything else rots. If you are writing a summary of the codebase, stop: that is what the
codebase is for. An over-full index is not a richer memory; past 22 KB it stops being read at all.

**The unit is one file per durable FACT, not one per work session.** This is the rule that
actually gets broken, and it is what fills an index: a busy project can reach dozens of memories in a few weeks
because each batch of work produced a memory about that batch. Signs you are writing a session log:

- the description opens with a date and a commit range
- it would be equally true to write "see commit abc123"
- it records that work *happened* rather than what the work *taught*

Before writing a new file, look for the one that should be edited instead (`--search` first when
unsure). Leave commit ranges out of the index line; git already answers that.

### When to write one

After a turn that established, changed or invalidated a **durable** fact. Most turns need none:
skip silently, and do not narrate the check. If a memory went stale or wrong, correct that file
rather than adding a second one that contradicts it. To retire one, set `status: superseded` and
move it into `archive/`. Link related memories from the body with `[[other-memory-name]]`.
